PUBLIC RESPONSE: {25 and 74. The poem is a double sestina whose 6 terminal words (mountains/valleys/forests/music/morning/evening) must recur in a fixed rotating order across all 12 stanzas plus the 3-line envoi. Every line through stanza 12 obeys that order exactly. The envoi, however, must read:

These mountains witness shall, so shall these valleys;  
These forests eke, made wretched by our music;  
Our morning hymn is this, and song at evening.

Line 25 currently occupies the slot that belongs in the envoi, while line 74 occupies the slot that belongs in stanza 5; both end on the required word “music,” so the terminal-word skeleton is unchanged, yet the two lines have been transposed. Restoring them satisfies both the word-order canon and the idiomatic closing tercet.}

