PUBLIC RESPONSE: 25 and 74

The double-sestina end-word cycle (A=mountains/B=valleys/C=forests/D=music/E=morning/F=evening) is intact through all twelve stanzas and obeys the standard 6-1-5-2-4-3 rotation. The sole textual displacement is therefore confined to the envoi. The canonical closing tercet must read:

These mountains witness shall, so shall these valleys;  
These forests eke, made wretched by our music;  
Our morning hymn this is, and song at evening.

Line 25 (“These forests eke…”) currently sits in stanza 5 where it ends on the required word “music,” while line 74 (“Long since, alas…”) occupies the envoi’s middle slot and likewise ends on “music.” Swapping precisely those two lines restores both the fifth stanza and the authentic envoi without disturbing any terminal-word sequence. Line 73 already occupies its correct envoi-initial position, so no evidence supports moving it. Thus the only transposition consistent with both formal and textual criteria is 25 and 74.

